+# FAQ for newcomers
+
+## How do I install the command line tool?
+
+You need Python 3.11 or newer. Install the tool with pipx: `pipx install tally-toolkit`. If the command is not found afterwards, run `pipx ensurepath` and open a new terminal.
+
+## What is the tool called now?
+
+`tallyctl`. It was called `tally-cli` until the 2026-09 release. The old name still works as an alias for one more release, but use the new name.
+
+## Which port does tally listen on?
+
+Port 8731. The old port 8080 is closed, so anything still pointing at it gets a refused connection.
+
+## How do I clear the cache?
+
+Run `tallyctl cache clear`. It clears all regions; add `--region NAME` to clear just one. The old purge command has been removed.
+
+## How long until an unanswered page escalates?
+
+15 minutes. If nobody acknowledges a page within 15 minutes, it goes to the next person.
+
+## Does the pager wake me at night?
+
+Only for severity 1, meaning customer-facing data loss or a full outage. Between 22:00 and 07:00, severity 2 incidents don't page; the primary picks them up in the morning. The pager app is Beacon: install it and log in with SSO before your first shift.
+
+## What do I do when the ingest queue is stuck?
+
+Run `tallyctl restart ingest --drain` first and look for the cause afterwards. The restart finishes in-flight work, parks the stuck batch in the dead-letter table and starts the consumer clean.
+
+## What do I do when the API certificate has expired?
+
+Renew the certificate with `tallyctl certs renew api`, then restart the API with `tallyctl restart api`. The restart is required, because the old certificate is served until you restart. You can check expiry dates with `tallyctl certs list`.
